Jack Bolsen wrote:
> I have read from some people that it doesn't pay to raise your AC past a
> certain point. I have always thought that extremely low ACs raise damage
> reduction accordingly, so there really isn't any such thing as an AC
> that is too low.
>
> I have had wizards who achieved -51 AC - is this overkill? At what point
> is getting a better AC just a waste of time?
>
> Jack

Once you get below 0 AC, according to recent messages (and corrections)
around these parts, benefits become based on a random AC between 0 and
the real armor class.  You also gain some damage reduction (figured by
the same formula).

This means, on a given hit, there's an equal chance of the hit being
against AC 0 and AC (whatever absurdly low figure you've earned for
yourself), as well as each point inbetween.  The lower your AC is, the
greater the potential advantage per attack, and the more likely the
number will be "low enough" to avoid the attack or take no damage from
it, but that probability never gets to zero.

I've won with ACs higher (that is, worse) than -20.  -51 is probably
overkill, but it still helps.  Towards the end, so long as you don't
YASD, the list of monsters that are a reasonable danger is usually
pretty small, and many of those will be on a non-conduct-player's
genocide list.

